java_code:
public int rangeSum(int[] nums, int n, int left, int right) {
    int MOD = (int)1e9 + 7;
    int[] sums = new int[n * (n + 1) / 2];
    int index = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {
            sum += nums[j];
            sums[index++] = sum;
        }
    }
    Arrays.sort(sums);
    int result = 0;
    for (int i = left - 1; i < right; ++i) {
        result = (result + sums[i]) % MOD;
    }
    return result;
}

java_description:
The algorithm implemented for this problem does the following:

1. Create a variable `MOD` holding the value 1e9 + 7;
2. Create a vector, array or list called `sums` which will store the sum of all non-empty continuous subarrays;
3. Iterate through `nums`, and for each position `i`, create another loop from `i` to `n - 1`, adding the elements to create the sum of the current subarray and appending or inserting it into `sums`.
4. Sort `sums` in non-decreasing order using `sort()` or `Arrays.sort()`. For Python and Javascript, you might need to implement a compare function;
5. Iterate through `sums` from index `left - 1` to `right - 1`, accumulating the desired subarray sums in variable `result`. Make sure to apply the modulo operation to the result in each addition;
6. Return the result.

cpp_code:
int rangeSum(vector<int>& nums, int n, int left, int right) {
    const int MOD = 1e9 + 7;
    vector<int> sums(n * (n + 1) / 2);
    int index = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {
            sum += nums[j];
            sums[index++] = sum;
        }
    }
    sort(sums.begin(), sums.end());
    int result = 0;
    for (int i = left - 1; i < right; ++i) {
        result = (result + sums[i]) % MOD;
    }
    return result;
}

